Dear Adam Berkeš,
Thank you for the report!

There are many opportunities for integer overflow in `fisher.test`:

```
// src/library/stats/src/fexact.c
// fexact():
    ntot = 0;
    for (i = 0; i < nrow; ++i) {
	for (j = 0; j < ncol; ++j) {
	    if (table[i + j * ldtabl] < 0) // line 212
		prterr(2, "All elements of TABLE must be nonnegative.");
	    ntot += table[i + j * ldtabl]; // line 214
// ...
    kk = k * nco; // line 234
// ...
    i1  = iwork(iwkmax, &iwkpt, ntot + 1, i_real); // line 236
// ...
    int ikh = imax2(k * 5 + (kk << 1), nco * 7 + 4*n2_stack); // line 242
// ...
    /* Compute row marginals and total */
    ntot = 0;
    for (i = 1; i <= nrow; ++i) {
	iro[i] = 0;
	for (j = 1; j <= ncol; ++j) {
	    if (table[i + j * ldtabl] < 0.)
		prterr(2, "All elements of TABLE must be nonnegative.");

	    iro[i] += table[i + j * ldtabl]; // line 422
	}
	ntot += iro[i];
// ...
    /* Column marginals */
    for (i = 1; i <= ncol; ++i) {
	ico[i] = 0;
	for (j = 1; j <= nrow; ++j)
	    ico[i] += table[j + i * ldtabl]; // line 438
    }
// ...
// f2xact():
	    for (i = 1, ii = j * ldtabl + 1; i <= nro; i++, ii++) {
		dd += fact[table[ii]]; // line 503
// ...
// iwork():
    if (itype == 2 || itype == 3)
	*iwkpt += number; // line 1838
    else { /* double */
	if (i % 2 != 0)
	    ++i;
	*iwkpt += (number << 1); // line 1842, originally reported overflow
```

`ntot` is used as an allocation size and overflows to a small number:
```
> fisher.test(matrix(2^30+1, 2, 4))
../../../../../src/library/stats/src/fexact.c:214:11: runtime error: signed integer overflow: 1073741825 + 1073741825 cannot be represented in type 'int'
../../../../../src/library/stats/src/fexact.c:422:13: runtime error: signed integer overflow: 1073741825 + 1073741825 cannot be represented in type 'int'
../../../../../src/library/stats/src/fexact.c:438:13: runtime error: signed integer overflow: 1073741825 + 1073741825 cannot be represented in type 'int'
```
...so the following memory access to the buffer is out of bounds:
```
==21661== Invalid read of size 8
==21661==    at 0xC6D5B57: f2xact (fexact.c:503)
==21661==    by 0xC6D5B57: fexact (fexact.c:291)
...
==21661==  Address 0x20d539b98 is not stack'd, malloc'd or (recently) free'd
```

Long matrices overflow the offset calculation:

```
> fisher.test(matrix(1L, 2^31-1, 2)) # requires 48G of memory! or very fast swap and patience
../../../../../src/library/stats/src/fexact.c:212:18: runtime error: signed integer overflow: 2147483647 + 1 cannot be represented in type 'int'
=================================================================
==11078==ERROR: AddressSanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow on address 0x7f193419b828 at pc 0x7f1f36b9cad3 bp 0x7ffdd6ecd140 sp 0x7ffdd6ecd138
READ of size 4 at 0x7f193419b828 thread T0
    #0 0x7f1f36b9cad2 in fexact ../../../../../src/library/stats/src/fexact.c:212
```

Other size calculations can overflow as well:

```
> fisher.test(matrix(c(1L, 0L, 0L), 2, 2^31/3), workspace = .Machine$integer.max)
../../../../../src/library/stats/src/fexact.c:234:8: runtime error: signed integer overflow: 715827882 * 715827885 cannot be represented in type 'int'
../../../../../src/library/stats/src/fexact.c:1838:9: runtime error: signed integer overflow: 715827882 + 1670265060 cannot be represented in type 'int'
../../../../../src/library/stats/src/fexact.c:242:44: runtime error: signed integer overflow: 715827882 * 7 cannot be represented in type 'int'
../../../../../src/library/stats/src/fexact.c:242:33: runtime error: left shift of 1670265058 by 1 places cannot be represented in type 'int'
../../../../../src/library/stats/src/fexact.c:242:23: runtime error: signed integer overflow: 715827885 * 5 cannot be represented in type 'int'
```

```
> fisher.test(matrix(c(2^30,2^30-1,0,0,0,0), 2, 3))
../../../../../src/library/stats/src/fexact.c:236:11: runtime error: signed integer overflow: 2147483647 + 1 cannot be represented in type 'int'
```

I've modified the function to be more careful about its allocation size calculations (tested with `make check-devel` on Linux). Do you think this is sufficient?
